    "q-180262": {
      "dom_id": "step-q180262",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If $-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}=4$, then $x=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the fractional term $-\\frac{2x}{3}$ by subtracting $\\frac{5}{2}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{5}{2} & =4 \\\\ -\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2x}{3} & =\\frac{8-5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2x}{3} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2x}{3} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so we multiply by its reciprocal ${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{-1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{2x}{3}) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1x & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
